Calc: the .xlsx export keeps plain text cells as packed runs of their row (8 bytes per cell instead of a record).

The export created one XclExpLabelCell record per text cell and held them all until the sheet was written. For a
50 MB workbook with 5.3 million text cells that was about 480 MB at the peak of saving (with the record slots in
the rows), on top of the loaded document. For .xlsx a finished text cell needs only its shared string index and
its cell format identifier: that is all XclExpLabelCell::SaveXml writes.

Each text cell is still created as an XclExpLabelCell, so the string table, fonts and cell formats are registered
as before and in the same order; the finished record is then replaced by a run entry, and adjacent entries of a
row merge through the existing TryMerge hook. The run writes, cell by cell, exactly the elements and attributes of
XclExpLabelCell::SaveXml. The row finalisation treats a run as non-blank cells, like the single text cells, so the
default row format, the blank cells and the dimensions are unchanged. Not used for the binary formats, for the
base cell of a merged range or for formatted (edit) text.
New logic in sc/source/filter/inc/hstxlsxpackedlabels.hxx; hooks: 7 lines in xetable.cxx and xetable.hxx.
The environment variable HST_SC_XLSX_PACKED_LABELS_OFF keeps one record per text cell (used by the test to compare
both paths).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new file is LicenseRef-HST-Proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_export_test6 testXlsxPackedTextCellsWriteTheSameSheet saves one document on both
paths (runs interrupted by numbers, a formula, an edit cell and a formatted blank cell, bold text, multi-line text,
a merged range, a hidden row, a long row) and compares sheet1.xml, sharedStrings.xml, styles.xml and workbook.xml;
the whole sc export suites (subsequent_export_test1..6) run on the new path; the large fixtures are saved on both
paths and compared part by part.
